# AI economy bias (user design, 2026-07-17): virtual, amortized per-minute
# consumption of the NON-CONSTANT costs — building construction, unit
# recruitment, tower ammunition — added to the consumption telemetry the AI
# senses (never to the real telemetry the HUD shows). The AI works to keep
# production >= consumption, so this bias forces it to stand up and SUSTAIN
# production of these resources instead of stalling once its starting stock
# runs dry (playtest 2026-07-17: roads stopped dead when STONE ran out).
#
# Values are hand-tuned per-minute averages. Every difficulty uses the same
# planning targets and decision quality; difficulty does not change the
# starting inventory or manpower of the AI.
#
# Retuned 2026-07-18 (AI economy tuning plan, Task 5) after fixing the three
# structural bugs the original values were masking (chain-walk poisoned by
# the bias, no credit for producers already under construction, road-tile
# reservations blocking corridor reuse) — the earlier 12/10/8/4/2 values
# never got a fair test since raising them (to 80/60/80/40/20 as a diagnostic
# experiment) couldn't fix a chain-walk bug that redirected every producer
# choice back to raw WOOD regardless of bias size. These are a fresh
# starting point, not re-validated against the old ones. Target: the bias
# is reachable in ~5-10 minutes of buildout (production catches up, urgency
# drops, the AI moves on to militaries) - re-tune via the behavior harness
# (tests/AIBehaviorHarnessTests.cpp)'s planks/m column if it stalls forever
# (bias too high) or the AI never bothers building the chain (bias too low).
ai_economy_bias
    difficulty_scale 1.0 1.0 1.0 1.0
    consumption WOOD 20
    consumption STONE 25
    consumption PLANKS 12
    consumption IRON 6
    consumption TOOLS 3
    consumption FOOD_PROVISIONS 12
    consumption ARROWS 6

    # Build-order priority (user design, 2026-07-19): weights DiagnoseResourceNeed's
    # urgency (0..PriorityCeiling=100, normalized before use) so the AI's FIRST
    # decisions favor the basic economy instead of a tie decided by ResourceType's
    # raw enum value. Root cause this fixes: with no producer yet, the urgency
    # formula gives WOOD/STONE/PLANKS/IRON/TOOLS/FOOD_PROVISIONS an IDENTICAL
    # 0.9 regardless of their `consumption` bias size (the formula is a ratio,
    # (consumed-produced)/consumed, which is always 1.0 when produced=0) — so
    # the tie used to go to whichever enum value was numerically lowest (IRON=3),
    # building a Foundry before a single Woodcutter. `priority` breaks that tie
    # by DESIGN. A resource with no line here defaults to the ceiling (full
    # weight, no penalty) — additive/opt-in.
    #
    # Tier 1 (basic economy — wood/stone/food chains): full weight.
    priority WOOD 100
    priority PLANKS 100
    priority STONE 100
    priority WHEAT 100
    priority FLOUR 100
    priority BREAD 100
    priority MEAT 100
    priority WATER 100
    priority FOOD_PROVISIONS 100
    # Tier 2 (iron/tools/weapon chain — Foundry/Smith/Barracks recruit costs):
    # suppressed until tier 1 has producers. NOTE (2026-07-20): "their own
    # urgency naturally drops once tier 1 does" never actually happened on its
    # own — the tier-1 consumption bias above is permanent, so tier-1 urgency
    # never really goes away. The real handoff is now explicit code
    # (UtilityAIModel::Sense's `economyEstablished` flag, AIModel.cpp) — once
    # `tower_readiness_buildings` production buildings stand and food is
    # alive, these weights stop being applied at all (full 1.0 everywhere).
    # These values only matter before that point, i.e. purely as an OPENING
    # build-order tiebreaker.
    # No standalone `consumption COAL` line (2026-07-20, harness catch):
    # Foundry's own recipe already lists `input COAL 1` alongside IRON_ORE
    # (buildings.rtsdata), so TryBuildProducerFor's chain-walk finds COAL as a
    # missing input on its own once IRON is targeted — no bias needed to
    # surface it. A `consumption COAL` line WAS tried and made COAL a
    # permanent standalone deficit candidate from tick 1 (pushed every cycle
    # via the "every amortized-cost resource is a candidate" loop in Sense),
    # which competed with the deterministic opening bootstrap before a single
    # Woodcutter existed and wedged the whole run (roster frozen, zero
    # deploys, AIBehaviorHarnessTests). `priority COAL 40` stays — it's inert
    # until COAL becomes a real candidate (chain-walk or actual consumption),
    # and suppresses it the same as the other tier-2 entries until then.
    priority COAL 40
    priority IRON 40
    priority TOOLS 40
    priority IRON_SWORD 40
    priority BRONZE_SWORD 40
    priority STEEL_SWORD 40

    # Tower build-order gate (user design, 2026-07-19): minimum completed
    # production buildings before the Defense need starts wanting a garrison
    # at all — "in the meantime" once the economy has SOME footing, not
    # gated behind the full tier-2 chain. Not a `priority` line since towers
    # aren't a resource.
    tower_readiness_buildings 4

    # Retry interval after no command was produced. A successful command uses
    # the shared difficulty cadence from ai/AIDifficulty.h; this value only
    # controls how often an infeasible candidate is reconsidered.
    decision_interval 1.0

    # Manpower floor (user design 2026-07-19): below this, with villages
    # already near population capacity (won't recover manpower on their own)
    # and the food chain alive, building another Village becomes the AI's
    # immediate priority (EconomySustain jumps to 0.9, above its normal 0.75
    # cap) instead of waiting for manpower to run out.
    manpower_reserve 20
end
